In article <CssIxz.GnA@biles.com> daniels@biles.com (Brad Daniels) writes:
>In article <2vroed$s2b@materna.materna.de>,
>Stefan Zimmermann <Stefan.Zimmermann@Materna.DE> wrote:
>...
>>I'm looking for DCE Threads implementations.

>>Especially I'm are looking for a solution for AIX.

>>I know Isis (but hav'nt a contact to the company), but I don't know, if
>>Isis exist for AIX
>>and I know that IBM have a Pthreads implementation. But we must be sure
>>that the sources we have to implement with DCE Threads are portable.

>DCE threads are just POSIX threads, or close enough as to make no difference.
>I believe they are specified in POSIX 1003.4, Real Time Extensions.  If you
>code to the OSF Application Development Guide/Reference, you should be fine
>from a portability standpoint.  The pthread_ routines in section one of
>the DCE reference manual are what you want.  Our code using those routines
>runs fine on VMS, OSF/1 and HP/UX, so it's pretty portable stuff.

However, it would be good to have a copy of the IEEE Posix threads
spec (draft 8, which I think is the latest) in hand when you use this.
Since DCE threads are draft 4, they aren't in complete agreement with
what is likely to emerge as the final Posix definition, so you might
want to avoid interfaces that have changed, or at least be aware of
them.

DCE now (unfortunately, in my jaded view) includes a specification for
its threads that is distinctly different from the Posix spec in some
areas.  I have no idea how these differences will be worked out, but,
frankly, I'm not optimistic.

