Alasdair Arthur writes:

>Does anybody have any experience of and/or views on the Netwise RPC product, 
>and in particular whether or not it can interoperate with DCE services?

Here at Mead Data, we use both DCE services and Netwise RPC.  There is no
defined interoperability between Netwise RPC and DCE RPC -- they use a 
completely different on the wire protocol.  However, we have (out of necessity)
built gateways between the two and in one instance, we have a client app.
that can make outbound Netwise or DCE RPC calls depending on which server
it needs to talk to.

My advice: Don't do this unless you have some compelling need to interoperate
           with existing Netwise apps.

>We are planning to adopt a DCE strategy, but while we go through the process of
>establishing the infrastructure etc., there are some applications which won't
>wait. Netwise appears to have the attraction of at least conforming
>to a DCE-like architecture, even if it doesn't have the security, CDS etc. The
>plan would be to use Netwise as a tactical solution, then replace it with DCE
>when we are ready.

Why not adopt the DCE services gradually instead?  Using just DCE RPC and
DCE Pthreads today with well known endpoints (via string bindings) or well-known hosts (via the rpcd endpoint mapper) will yield far superior performance (assuming your servers are multithreaded) than you will ever attain with 
Netwise RPC (See gotcha note below). We know this from experience.

With this approach, you defer having to build a DCE cell until you really
need CDS and security.

The only gotcha with this approach is that you have to deal with threads
and in particular with non-thread-safe libraries.  The very conservative
approach to this problem is to have clients make completely synchronous RPC
calls (as opposed to creating a separate thread to issue the call) and
if necessary having single client servers.  You minimize the pain from
lack of thread-safe code, but you also give up most of the DCE RPC performance
advantage over Netwise RPC.

If you are developing in C, then most of the major vendors provide a reasonable set of thread-tolerant libraries (with the exception of Xt and Motif); 
however, the only platform we've found so far that is safe for threaded C++ apps is Solaris 2.3.  HP-UX will get much better with 10.0; I don't know the
status of thread-safe libraries on DEC OSF/1, AIX, etc.

