In article <2upn1l$7ho@news1.svc.portal.com>, urs@shell.portal.com (Urs - Rufer) writes:
|>Is there a debugger available somewhere (preferably via ftp) for OSF v1.3 or
|>v2.0 on alpha that's a little more user-friendly than dbx?
|>

Digital offers two debuggers as part of the base operating system: dbx and 
DECladebug.  There is a Motif GUI that can be used with dbx or DECladebug.
The GUI has multiple views, a button bar, a natural, easy-to-use interface,
and good performance.  The GUI is customizable and can be used either 
stand-alone or as part of the FUSE environment.  (The GUI is replacing the
current FUSE Debugger GUI.)  There is also a command window.

Both dbx and DECladebug are in the final stages of field test for 
the next Digital OSF release. Both products have dramatic improvements over 
the previous versions and represent a significant effort over the last 12 
months (over 10 man-years of effort in the case of DECladebug). 

DECladebug is Digital's newest technology for debugging. It is an object 
oriented (C++) framework for the development of debugging tools. (There was
a paper presented at last year's OOPSLA conference with more detail, if you're
interested.)  DECladebug provides an alternative to dbx for program debugging.  
The primary difference between DECladebug and dbx is the level of language 
debugging; DECladebug has C++ (where dbx doesn't) and DECladebug has more 
Fortran, COBOL, and Ada support than does dbx. (At the moment, DECladebug does 
not provide kernel debugging capabilities.)  For those, users familiar with 
dbx command syntax, DECladebug will feel natural. Digital is gradually 
migrating to the use of DECladebug.

The new release of DECladebug will also include:

	- Significant language enhancements for Fortran, Fortran-90.
	- Continued leadership support for C++ (templates, exceptions,
	  multiple inheritance, and overloaded functions and operators).
	- Support for Kernel and CMA thread debugging
	- Additional features to support Ada and COBOL debugging.
	- Source-level remote debugging support for Alpha/OSF systems.
	- Dramatic improvements in performance.
	- A significant expansion of the size of applications that can be
	  run under the debugger.
	- Attach (and detach) to a running process.
	- Real*16 support
	- Xtaso (short pointer) support for very large applications

All these features will be shipped in the operating system packages in the
upcoming OSF release.  DECladebug will be backwards compatible with the
OSF V2.0 OS release.

The dbx version that is coming out with this next OSF version is expected
to include: 

	- Real*16 support
	- Support for Kernel and CMA thread debugging
	- Attach and detach from a running process
	- Debug across fork call
	- Xtaso (short pointer) support for very large applications

So - you have a couple of alternatives, and you should check out the new
GUI.  Contact your Digital representative for more information.
